    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for my ground school exam and I'm a bit confused about lift. Can you explain how Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law actually work together to create lift on a wing? I want to make sure I understand the physics behind it, not just the definitions.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Bernoulli's principle explains lift by comparing the pressure above and below an airfoil such as a wing. The design of a wing causes the air flowing over the top surface to speed up more than the air flowing underneath, resulting in lower pressure above the wing compared to below. This pressure difference creates an upward force, which is lift. Meanwhile, Newton's third law comes into play as well. According to this law, for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. As a wing pushes air down and back, this creates a reaction that pushes the wing upward, complementing the pressure difference created by Bernoulli's principle. So, Bernoulli's principle explains the difference in pressure, and Newton's third law explains the reaction lift that occurs due to the wing pushing against the air. Both concepts are essential to understanding how airplanes are able to fly.",
      "source": "target",
